<CounterfeitStudy
  title="Austerities and the Commanded Fast"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Thirty-First of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="One day Yahuah commanded, and all the days men added."
  description="The thirty-first of the 38 Blessings, self-made austerity, set beside the one fast Yahuah commanded and the Sabbath Isaiah joined to it."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>The thirty-first blessing, "Exercising austerities," comes from the Pali <em>tapo</em>, a word that literally means "heat." The idea is that hard self-denial burns away craving the way fire burns off dross. Buddhist monks keep many such rules: no food after midday, little sleep, long hours of sitting meditation, and the refusal of comfort.</p>
    <p>The record is worth noting. Buddhist tradition says the Buddha himself first tried extreme self-starvation, nearly died, and turned away from it to what he called the "Middle Way." Yet austerity still stands on the list of blessings.</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Yahuah did command affliction. On Yom Kippur, Israel was to "afflict your souls" (Leviticus 23:27-32), and fasting appears alongside prayer throughout Scripture (Ezra 8:21; Daniel 9:3). Yahushua said "when ye fast," not "if" (Matthew 6:16-18). So self-denial has a real root in the Word.</p>
    <p>Isaiah 58 shows what Yahuah thinks of fasting done the wrong way. Israel complained, "Wherefore have we fasted... and thou seest not?" (Isaiah 58:3). Yahuah answered that the fast He chose was "to loose the bands of wickedness" and "to deal thy bread to the hungry" (Isaiah 58:6-7). In the same chapter He joins that fast to the Sabbath: turning from "doing thy pleasure on my holy day" and calling "the sabbath a delight" (Isaiah 58:13-14). The commanded fast and the commanded rest stand side by side.</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>Yahuah's affliction is a set day He commanded, aimed at turning back to Him and doing right by others. Ascetic austerity is a program of hardship a man designs for himself, aimed at putting out his own desire.</p>
    <p>Paul names it plainly: "Touch not; taste not; handle not," rules "after the commandments and doctrines of men," which have "a shew of wisdom in will worship, and humility, and neglecting of the body; not in any honour to the satisfying of the flesh" (Colossians 2:21-23). Paul is not speaking against Yahuah's Law there. He names the source of the rules he rejects: "the commandments and doctrines of men" (Colossians 2:22).</p>
    <h2>The Same Turn in Christian Circles</h2>
    <p>Rome built a calendar of austerity Torah never gave: the forty days of Lent, in place by the fourth century, meatless Fridays, vows of poverty, and, in some orders, whipping one's own body. At the same time it set aside the one fast Yahuah did command, Yom Kippur, and the Sabbath Isaiah joined to it.</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Yahuah commanded one day a year to afflict the soul, six days to work, and the seventh to rest and delight in Him. Men added the rest, and the added rules have "a shew of wisdom" and no power.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Scripture's affliction is a day Yahuah commanded; ascetic austerity is "will worship" after "the commandments and doctrines of men" (Colossians 2:22-23).</p>
    <p>Revelation says where the blessing truly lies: "Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life" (Revelation 22:14). Not self-made heat, but His commandments.</p>
